document.getElementById('delete-modal').addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.key === 'Tab' && !e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === focusable[focusable.length - 1]) {
    e.preventDefault();
    focusable[0].focus();
  }
  if (e.key === 'Tab' && e.shiftKey && document.activeElement === focusable[0]) {
    e.preventDefault();
    focusable[focusable.length - 1].focus();
  }
});
```

This modal correctly traps Tab/Shift+Tab between its two buttons, and correctly restores focus to the trigger on close — the developer clearly tested basic tabbing. But a keyboard-only user still can't fully operate this dialog the way a mouse user can. Do a full review: identify every remaining keyboard-accessibility problem, explain why each is invisible to someone testing only with a mouse or only checking that the two buttons wrap correctly, and say which one you'd fix first and why.
